  "answer": "### Bug Identification\nThe bug is an **off-by-one error** in the loop condition. The loop uses \u0060i \u003C= values.Length\u0060, which causes the index to increment one step beyond the last valid element of the array. In C#, array indices range from \u00600\u0060 to \u0060values.Length - 1\u0060. When \u0060i\u0060 equals \u0060values.Length\u0060, accessing \u0060values[i]\u0060 throws an \u0060IndexOutOfRangeException\u0060.\n\n### Explanation of Empty-Array Behavior\nIf the input array is empty (\u0060values.Length == 0\u0060):\n1. The loop condition checks if \u00600 \u003C= 0\u0060, which is true, so the loop body executes once.\n2. Inside the loop, it attempts to access \u0060values[0]\u0060.\n3. Since the array has no elements (length 0), index \u00600\u0060 is out of bounds.\n4. This results in an immediate \u0060IndexOutOfRangeException\u0060 being thrown before any summation occurs.\n\n### Minimal Fix\nChange the loop condition from \u0060i \u003C= values.Length\u0060 to \u0060i \u003C values.Length\u0060. This ensures the loop runs only for valid indices (\u00600\u0060 through \u0060values.Length - 1\u0060).\n\n**Corrected Code:**\n\u0060\u0060\u0060csharp\nstatic int Sum(int[] values)\n{\n    int total = 0;\n    // Fixed: Changed \u003C= to \u003C to prevent index out of range\n    for (int i = 0; i \u003C values.Length; i\u002B\u002B) \n    {\n        total \u002B= values[i];\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n\u0060\u0060\u0060",
